---
import Landing from '../layouts/Landing.astro';
import { MAX_NOTE_BYTES } from '../lib/limits';
import type { Faq } from '../lib/schema';

const faqs: Faq[] = [
  {
    q: 'Can I send a whole .env file?',
    a: `Yes. Paste it in: a note can hold up to ${MAX_NOTE_BYTES / 1024} KB of text, which fits most .env files. With a free account you can also attach the file itself, up to 5 MB, encrypted the same way.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Why not paste keys into Slack, Teams or a ticket?',
    a: 'Because they stay there: in the history, in search, in exports, and in every integration that can read the channel. Secrets pasted in chat are one of the most common ways keys leak.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Shouldn’t we use a secrets manager?',
    a: 'Yes, for anything your systems use. A one-time link is for the hand-off: getting a key to a person who will put it into the secrets manager, a CI setting or their local .env. It’s the step where keys usually end up in chat.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can someone send a key to me?',
    a: 'Yes. With an account, create an information request and send its link. Whatever they enter is encrypted in their browser to a key only you hold, so they don’t need an account and we can’t read it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a line break or a space get changed?',
    a: 'No. The note is encrypted and decrypted exactly as written, including line breaks, spaces and quotes, and shown as plain text.',
  },
];
---

<Landing
  title="Share API keys and .env files securely"
  description="Send an API key, token or .env file to a teammate with a link that works once. Encrypted in your browser, never left in Slack or Git. Free."
  eyebrow="For developers"
  heading="Share API keys and .env files without leaving them in Slack."
  lead="Paste the key or the whole .env below and send the one-time link. Your teammate copies it into place, and the only copy we ever held, which we couldn’t read, is deleted."
  points={[
    `Up to ${MAX_NOTE_BYTES / 1024} KB of text: enough for most .env files`,
    'Line breaks, spaces and quotes kept exactly',
    'Nothing left in chat history, tickets or Git',
  ]}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <h2>Where keys leak</h2>
  <p>
    Rarely through clever attacks. Usually a key was pasted into a chat channel, a ticket, an email or a commit “just for
    a minute”, and stayed there. Chat history is searchable, exported and readable by every bot in the channel. Git
    history keeps a secret even after the line is deleted.
  </p>

  <h2>A safer hand-off</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste the key, token or <code>.env</code> contents into the form. Your browser encrypts it.</li>
    <li>Set it to destroy after one view and expire in 1 hour if it isn’t opened.</li>
    <li>Send the link in the chat. Once your teammate has opened it, the link in the history is useless.</li>
    <li>They put the value into your secrets manager, CI settings or their local <code>.env</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If the link didn’t work for them, someone else opened it first. Rotate the key. That is also why one view is the
    default: you find out.
  </p>

  <h2>Built so the key never reaches us</h2>
  <p>
    Encryption is AES-256-GCM with the Web Crypto API built into the browser. The key is in the link’s fragment, after the{' '}
    <code>#</code>, which browsers don’t send to servers. We don’t log request bodies, and the pages where notes are
    written and read load no third-party scripts. Details are in our <a href="/security">security model</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Generating a new secret?</h2>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/password-generator">password generator</a> makes random strings in your browser, up to 64 characters,
    and can drop the result straight into a note.
  </p>
</Landing>
